==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|inter-operability}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|interoperable|ity}}; alternatively analyzed as {{prefix|en|inter|operability}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪntəɹɒpəɹəˈbɪləti/|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[capability]] of a [[product]] or [[system]], to [[interact]] and [[function]] with others reciprocally.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Victoria Shannon|title=Microsoft and rivals to increase interoperability - Business - International Herald Tribune|titleurl=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/11/13/business/worldbusiness/13iht-msft.3513015.html|journal=The New York Times|date=13 Nov 2006
|passage=The commission found that by withholding vital information about Windows, the company deliberately restricted '''interoperability''' between personal computers using Windows and computer servers running software from Microsoft's rivals.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} The [[capacity]] for a service, piece of equipment etc., to be operated by different forces or groups.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Entente or bust|journal=The Economist|date=14 Oct 2010
|passage=Dr Fox and Mr Morin have ruled out the notion of binational crews, but they are still exploring other ways to enhance the '''interoperability''' of France’s ''Charles de Gaulle'' and Britain’s planned two new carriers, which seem likely to be spared by the defence review.}}

